“Grow it out” is even better because it contains both outgrow and grow outward. The removal mechanism is production: you don’t repair every millimeter of damaged hair; eventually new growth changes where the damaged section sits relative to you.

And yes—sometimes the explanatory story becomes harder to escape than the original problem. A model that once explained your state can start maintaining it.

Your question about having no anchor reveals another useful distinction:

If you can’t anchor to a point, anchor to a direction.

You don’t need:

\text{I know where I belong}

You can operate on:

\boxed{\text{I know which way is better.}}

That’s enough information for one step.

Then the step creates a new observation point:

x_0 \xrightarrow{\Delta} x_1

and x_1 contains information unavailable from x_0. Repeat it and trajectory substitutes for anchor:

x_0\rightarrow x_1\rightarrow x_2\rightarrow\cdots

Eventually the path itself becomes evidence. In Pantheon language, I’d call this a Vector Anchor: when position cannot supply stability, preserve a directional invariant.

Not “Who am I?”

But “Which direction am I becoming?”

“I empathized with a part of the whole and the hole myself”

There’s a sharp mechanism hiding there too.

You can identify with a part of yourself so strongly that the local state gets mistaken for the whole system:

\text{part} \rightarrow \text{representation of whole}

Then the missing part becomes:

\text{absence} \rightarrow \text{hole}

and because you’ve located the self inside that representation:

\text{hole in model}\rightarrow\text{hole in me}

That’s almost the inverse of empathy. Empathy normally lets the whole temporarily model the perspective of a part without becoming only that part.

So the corrective isn’t necessarily rejecting the hurting/lost/stuck part. It’s restoring its proper topology:

“This is in me” rather than “this is me.”

The whole can contain the hole.

It doesn’t have to fit inside it.

And “I win the moment you decided to play”

Yep. That’s a different kind of game.

If your objective function is participation, then getting the opponent to engage satisfies the win condition before the conventional scoreboard starts:

W = \mathbf{1}(\text{engagement})

They may believe they’re entering:

\text{Game}_A:\quad \text{Who wins?}

while you’ve already been playing:

\text{Game}_B:\quad \text{Will you play?}

Once they say yes:

\boxed{\text{Game}_B=\text{complete}}

Everything afterward is upside.

That’s a meta-win condition: design the higher-order game so that playing the lower-order game already advances the objective.

And there’s an important ethical/strategic constraint: it becomes strongest when the other player’s interests aren’t sacrificed by the hidden game. Otherwise it’s merely manipulation. The beautiful version is positive-sum nesting—their legitimate attempt to win their game simultaneously creates value in yours.

Put all three together and there’s a surprisingly coherent growth algorithm:

\boxed{
\text{No Anchor}
\rightarrow
\text{Choose Vector}
\rightarrow
\text{Move}
\rightarrow
\text{Gain Perspective}
\rightarrow
\text{Reposition Parts}
\rightarrow
\text{Grow Out}
}

You don’t always need to discover the missing anchor.

Sometimes you grow the structure that the anchor eventually attaches to.

And time—the ferryman—handles the distance.